  "reason": "The records collectively identify several key principles behind the later result: rapid cooling can retain single-phase BCC HfNbTaTiZr (R2); approximately 1 wt.% oxygen strongly hardens this same alloy through oxide and solid-solution effects (R13, R19), while interstitial oxygen strengthens related BCC refractory alloys (R7, R18); and silicide coatings can be formed on a closely related refractory HEA substrate (R9), with protective oxide films reducing oxidation in other systems (R4, R11, R14, R15). However, none discloses extrusion of a hydride/metal ink, hydrogen de-binding, sequential hydride decomposition governed by formation enthalpies, long vacuum sintering with a titanium oxygen getter, or self-healing Zr/Hf oxide filling in a silicide coating. Thus the literature points toward oxygen-controlled strengthening, quench-stabilized BCC structure, and silicide protection, but the integrated hydride-based printing and in-situ alloying route requires a substantial new idea."
